  "id": "gr-desu",
  "connector": "です",
  "gloss": "is, am, are (polite)",
  "title": "です — X is Y",
  "pattern": "A は B です。 / A は B じゃないです。",
  "explanation": "です ends a polite sentence that says what something is: {私|わたし}は{学生|がくせい}です — I’m a student. It’s the same for I, you, he or they, and Japanese has no word for “a” or “the”. To say something isn’t so, use じゃないです (or the more formal ではありません). A question just adds か: {学生|がくせい}ですか — are you a student?",
  "natural": "です is the safe ending with anyone you don’t know well: staff, colleagues, a partner’s family. With friends and a partner, drop it, or use だ: {学生|がくせい}だよ.",
  "stiff": "Don’t put です after a verb: {行|い}きます, not {行|い}くです. And ending every sentence with だ sounds blunt to strangers.",
